<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The one scheduled job this module ships: drain the automation run queue.

    WHY IT EXISTS

    Every trigger adapter (#5752-#5756) does the same cheap, transactional thing:
    it writes a `turnstone.automation.run` row in state `queued`. None of them
    launches, because they all run inside somebody else's transaction (a form
    save, a customer's message post, an inbound HTTP request) and the launch
    service is an HTTP call of up to 120 seconds. This cron is what turns those
    queued intents into sessions, on its own thread, with nothing of the user's
    waiting behind it.

    WHY ITS INTERVAL IS A CONFIGURATION VALUE, NOT THIS FILE'S NUMBER

    `interval_number` below is the default. The setting that decides it is
    `turnstone_integration.drain_interval_minutes` in `ir.config_parameter`, and
    `_sync_drain_interval()` on the run model mirrors it onto this row at install
    time and whenever the settings form is saved. A number written only here
    would be a setting that quietly does nothing — an administrator would change
    the parameter and see no change until they found this file.

    That is also why the record is `noupdate="1"`: an upgrade must not overwrite
    the interval a deployment has configured. The mirroring method is what
    changes it afterwards.

    WHY `base.user_root`

    The cron runs as the superuser because it must be able to read every queued
    run and reach the connection's token to launch. It does NOT launch anything
    as the superuser: the rule's `run_as` setting decides whose identity each
    session belongs to, and `_resolve_run_as_user()` refuses rather than falling
    back to root. See `_launch_claimed_run()` on the run model.
-->
<odoo noupdate="1">
    <record id="ir_cron_turnstone_drain_runs" model="ir.cron">
        <field name="name">Turnstone: drain automation runs</field>
        <field name="model_id" ref="model_turnstone_automation_run"/>
        <field name="state">code</field>
        <field name="code">model._drain_queue()</field>
        <field name="user_id" ref="base.user_root"/>
        <field name="interval_number">1</field>
        <field name="interval_type">minutes</field>
        <field name="nextcall" eval="(DateTime.now() + timedelta(minutes=1)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"/>
        <field name="priority">5</field>
        <field name="active" eval="True"/>
    </record>

    <!--
        The schedule scanner. A separate cron rather than a second call in the
        drain, because the two answer different questions and a deployment may
        reasonably want different cadences for them: the drain is latency on
        events that have already happened (as often as possible), while the
        scanner walks a due-date index on a business model (as rarely as the
        shortest rule window needs). Keeping them separate also means a slow
        scan cannot delay draining an event somebody is waiting on.

        It is `active="False"` on install. A fresh install has no `schedule`
        rules, so there is nothing to scan; an administrator turns it on when
        they write the first one, and the rule's own form says so. Shipping a
        cron that walks a business model on every instance that installed the
        module, whether or not it uses schedules, is a cost nobody asked for.
    -->
    <record id="ir_cron_turnstone_run_schedules" model="ir.cron">
        <field name="name">Turnstone: fire due automation schedules</field>
        <field name="model_id" ref="model_turnstone_trigger_schedule"/>
        <field name="state">code</field>
        <field name="code">model._run_due_schedules()</field>
        <field name="user_id" ref="base.user_root"/>
        <field name="interval_number">5</field>
        <field name="interval_type">minutes</field>
        <field name="nextcall" eval="(DateTime.now() + timedelta(minutes=5)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"/>
        <field name="priority">10</field>
        <field name="active" eval="False"/>
    </record>
</odoo>
